WEBVTT

00:00:08,750 --> 00:00:13,550
There are a risk of disintegrating cancer cells,

00:00:13,550 --> 00:00:18,430
disintegrated cancer cells going somewhere else in the body and causing problems.

00:00:18,430 --> 00:00:24,150
What happens to the cancer cells that are destroyed using a rif machine?

00:00:24,150 --> 00:00:29,030
Good question. So, if the rif works,

00:00:29,030 --> 00:00:35,370
could you end up a cybertype reaction because only these cancer cells,

00:00:35,370 --> 00:00:47,470
and it's causing waste from these cancer cells to affect body?

00:00:47,470 --> 00:00:53,470
So, the short answer is this, no, this is not going to happen.

00:00:53,470 --> 00:00:57,750
And the reason is that the rif doesn't work,

00:00:57,750 --> 00:01:00,230
so there's different ways cells die.

00:01:00,230 --> 00:01:06,710
So cells can die from normal programmed cell death called apoptosis.

00:01:07,190 --> 00:01:10,790
So apoptosis takes place when a cell actually,

00:01:10,790 --> 00:01:15,110
actually the term is blabbed in on itself.

00:01:15,110 --> 00:01:21,430
So, the cell kind of inverts on itself and starts breaking in part.

00:01:21,430 --> 00:01:23,390
And it has actually recycled.

00:01:23,390 --> 00:01:27,630
So, let the cell material inside the cell,

00:01:27,630 --> 00:01:31,070
including the cell membrane, what makes it up.

00:01:31,070 --> 00:01:35,310
A cell membrane, the phospholipid bilayer is reutilized by the body,

00:01:35,310 --> 00:01:36,910
reutilized by other cells.

00:01:36,910 --> 00:01:40,510
So, it's actually really a recycling of cell material.

00:01:40,510 --> 00:01:47,350
So, what a cell is destroyed even through your T cells and your natural killer cells,

00:01:47,350 --> 00:01:54,510
they stimulate what's called an extrinsic from the outside apoptotic reaction.

00:01:54,510 --> 00:01:56,670
So, the cell goes through apoptosis.

00:01:56,670 --> 00:02:00,270
So, you don't end up with toxins.

00:02:00,310 --> 00:02:05,510
The cell doesn't explode and cause toxins to be dispersed

00:02:05,510 --> 00:02:09,590
in the extracellular matrix that need to be carried away by the lymph system.

00:02:11,190 --> 00:02:15,950
That takes place in a, what's called a pathological death of a cell.

00:02:15,950 --> 00:02:20,390
A pathological death of a cell is a process called cell lysis

00:02:20,390 --> 00:02:25,190
where the cell membrane does break apart and kind of explode.

00:02:25,190 --> 00:02:28,990
And it produces a lot of waste,

00:02:28,990 --> 00:02:33,070
which is called endotoxins, waste from within.

00:02:33,070 --> 00:02:37,870
And that has to be carried away through your lymphatic system

00:02:37,870 --> 00:02:41,230
because it makes a mess in the extracellular matrix.

00:02:41,230 --> 00:02:46,950
Matrix, that's the matrix of fluid and waste products between cells.

00:02:46,950 --> 00:02:49,830
And then that's carried away through your lymph system.

00:02:49,830 --> 00:02:53,390
And then it has to go through detox pathways.

00:02:53,390 --> 00:02:58,910
That is a problem when you use the rife for killing single cell organisms.

00:02:58,910 --> 00:03:01,710
So, if you're using the rife to kill bacteria,

00:03:01,710 --> 00:03:06,390
you can and you will produce endotoxins.

00:03:06,390 --> 00:03:09,630
So, you'll produce waste from that bacteria.

00:03:09,630 --> 00:03:14,390
Now, it's usually not a problem unless a person has huge issues

00:03:14,390 --> 00:03:16,830
with their detoxification pathways.

00:03:16,830 --> 00:03:20,110
Unless you're killing a certain type of bacteria

00:03:20,110 --> 00:03:21,710
called gram-negative bacteria.

00:03:21,710 --> 00:03:24,070
Gram-negative bacteria,

00:03:24,070 --> 00:03:25,950
Lyme disease, beryllia, burgodorphia,

00:03:25,950 --> 00:03:28,590
is a gram-negative bacteria at each part of the organism.

00:03:28,590 --> 00:03:32,190
The gram-negative bacteria, if you kill them both too quickly.

00:03:32,190 --> 00:03:35,070
The other thing with gram-negative bacteria is that they have

00:03:35,070 --> 00:03:38,230
lipopolysaccharides in their cell membranes.

00:03:38,230 --> 00:03:42,030
And those liberate and those can cause an inflammatory reaction

00:03:42,030 --> 00:03:45,270
cause what could be known as a herxymaraction.

00:03:45,270 --> 00:03:46,630
But to answer the question,

00:03:46,630 --> 00:03:50,790
when you're killing cancer cells using the rife,

00:03:50,790 --> 00:03:53,630
typically using any other natural approach,

00:03:53,630 --> 00:03:55,630
you're not going to cause a herxymaraction.

00:03:55,630 --> 00:03:59,790
You're not going to cause endotoxins to be produced.

00:03:59,790 --> 00:04:03,390
You're not going to overwhelm the detox pathways.

00:04:03,390 --> 00:04:06,550
So, I know that you will do that with chemotherapy.

00:04:06,550 --> 00:04:11,550
Chemotherapy kills cancer via lysis and through apoptosis.

00:04:12,150 --> 00:04:16,190
So, you can produce endotoxins when you use chemotherapy.

00:04:16,190 --> 00:04:19,830
That can be part of why a person feels ill

00:04:19,830 --> 00:04:21,710
post chemotherapy.

00:04:21,710 --> 00:04:23,990
Of course, the other reason is that chemotherapy

00:04:24,030 --> 00:04:26,550
isn't going to be exclusively killing cancer cells.

00:04:26,550 --> 00:04:29,310
It will be killing healthy cells too.

00:04:29,310 --> 00:04:31,070
It can damage the blood brain barrier,

00:04:31,070 --> 00:04:33,950
cross the blood barrier, cause inflammation of the brain,

00:04:33,950 --> 00:04:35,110
causing all sorts of things.

00:04:35,110 --> 00:04:38,950
So, while chemotherapy can be great to kill cancer,

00:04:38,950 --> 00:04:41,710
it causes other ill effects as well.

00:04:41,710 --> 00:04:42,710
So...

